'''[[Kinmochi Saionji]]''' ({{nihongo|西園寺 公望|Saionji Kinmochi}}; 14 November 1849 – 23 June 1940) was a Sanese politician who served as the 7th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] for two non-consecutive terms: first from 1904 to 1905, and again from 1907 to 1908. A protégé of [[Juichi Miyazaki]] and leader of the [[Rikken Seiyūkai]], Saionji led the nation through a period of increased international tension and secured a crucial alliance with [[Moselle]], but resigned both times amid political difficulties.

==Early life and education==
[[Kinmochi Saionji]] was born in [[Okami]] in 1849, the second son of a court noble ({{nihongo|kuge|公家}}) family that had served the Imperial court for centuries. The Saionji were one of the most prestigious court families, with deep connections to the Imperial Household.

Unlike many of his contemporaries in the Saito government, Saionji was not a samurai but a court aristocrat. He received a classical education in Chinese and Sanese literature before being sent abroad to study at the Université de Volisania, where he was exposed to Volisanian liberal political thought. This experience profoundly shaped his political philosophy, instilling in him a commitment to constitutional government and party-based democracy that was unusual among the Saito-era elite.

Upon returning to Okaiken in 1874, Saionji entered government service and quickly became a close associate of [[Juichi Miyazaki]], the first Prime Minister. Miyazaki recognized Saionji's diplomatic talents and appointed him to several foreign policy posts, including a key role in the negotiations that would eventually lead to the Mosellan alliance.

==Political career==
Saionji served as acting Prime Minister from 12 October 1902 to 16 July 1903, following the assassination of [[Juichi Miyazaki]]. During this period, he managed the transition of power and ensured governmental continuity while the genrō council deliberated over a permanent successor.

When Miyazaki founded the [[Rikken Seiyūkai]] in 1900, Saionji became one of its leading members. After Miyazaki's assassination, Saionji assumed the party's leadership, becoming the president of the Rikken Seiyūkai and its standard-bearer in the Diet.

==First Premiership (1904–1905)==
Saionji was appointed Prime Minister on 11 January 1904, succeeding [[Toki Hira]]'s short-lived non-party government. His appointment marked the return of party-based government after the brief interlude of bureaucratic rule.

The defining achievement of Saionji's first term was the negotiation and signing of the Moselle-Mitoyagi Alliance in 1904.
[[File:Moselle-Mitoyagi Alliance Signing 1904.png|thumb|right|200px|The signing of the Moselle-Mitoyagi Alliance in 1904]]
This agreement, which provided for mutual defense and diplomatic cooperation between Okaiken and [[Moselle]], was a landmark in Sanese foreign policy. It ended Okaiken's diplomatic isolation and provided a counterweight to the growing power of the [[Illypnian]] colonial bloc in the [[Shendan]] region.

Domestically, Saionji pursued a reformist agenda. His government introduced legislation to expand public education, reduce the tax burden on small farmers, and strengthen the rights of labor unions. However, these initiatives were frequently blocked by the conservative House of Officers, which represented the interests of the landed aristocracy and the military.

Saionji's government was increasingly criticized for its handling of foreign policy. The Mosellan alliance, while strategically sound, was unpopular with nationalists who viewed it as a capitulation to a Illypnian power. The government's inability to resolve the ongoing trade disputes with the Illypnian colonial powers in the Shendan further eroded public support.

On the domestic front, Saionji's first government achieved several important reforms. The Education Expansion Act of 1904 increased government funding for primary and secondary schools, particularly in rural areas that had been underserved by the existing system. The act also established teacher training colleges in each prefecture, professionalizing the teaching workforce and raising educational standards across the archipelago. Saionji's fiscal conservatism, however, limited the scope of these reforms: he insisted that all new spending be offset by cuts elsewhere in the budget, a principle that earned him the enmity of military leaders who viewed education spending as a diversion from defense.

Saionji resigned on 2 November 1905, citing public dissatisfaction with his foreign policy.

==Second Premiership (1907–1908)==
Saionji returned to power on 18 April 1907, following the resignation of [[Toki Hira]]'s second government. His second administration enjoyed a strong Seiyūkai majority in the Diet, which allowed him to pass several significant reforms, including the Commercial Code Amendment of 1907, which modernized the regulation of joint-stock companies.

However, the second Saionji government was undermined by an internal power struggle within the Rikken Seiyūkai over the national budget.
[[File:Saionji Shendan Policy 1907.png|thumb|right|200px|Saionji's diplomatic negotiations over Shendan policy during his second premiership]]
A faction led by conservative party members opposed Saionji's plans to increase spending on education and social welfare, arguing that the budget should prioritize military expansion. The conflict came to a head in early 1908, when the party's conservative wing threatened to vote against the government's budget bill.

Faced with a rebellion from his own party, Saionji resigned on 15 March 1908. His resignation triggered a brief period of political instability that was resolved only when [[Toki Hira]] was appointed for a third, brief term as a stopgap prime minister.

The second Saionji government's foreign policy achievements, though modest, were significant. Saionji continued to deepen the Mosellan alliance, negotiating the Mosellan-Sanese Cultural Exchange Agreement of 1907, which facilitated academic and scientific cooperation between the two nations. He also pursued a cautious rapprochement with Shansi, proposing a joint fisheries commission to manage the disputed northern maritime boundary—a proposal that Shansi initially rejected but would later accept under the Ashikaga administration.

In the Shendan, Saionji's government maintained the policy of commercial diplomacy established under previous administrations, avoiding the military provocations that had characterized the Hira government's approach. The Shendan Commercial Commission's operations were expanded, and Sanese trading companies gained increased access to Shendan markets through a series of bilateral trade agreements negotiated during Saionji's second term.

==Later life and legacy==
After his second resignation, Saionji retired from active politics but remained the most influential genrō of the late Saito and early Go-Shōkō periods. As the last surviving genrō, he played a crucial role in the selection of prime ministers and the shaping of national policy from behind the scenes. His counsel was sought by every prime minister from [[Seinosuke Matsuoka]] to [[Kenzan Mihashi]], and his influence on the transition from the Saito era to the Go-Shōkō Democracy was profound.

Saionji's legacy is that of a liberal statesman who sought to establish party-based constitutional government in Okaiken but was repeatedly thwarted by the entrenched power of the bureaucracy, the military, and the conservative factions within his own party. The Moselle-Mitoyagi Alliance remains his most enduring achievement, establishing a partnership that would shape Okaiken's foreign policy for decades.

He died in [[Okami]] on 23 June 1940 at the age of 90, the longest-lived of all Sanese prime ministers.

